            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> The particular steps you reported, are an interesting case, because the \"delimiters\" of the insert-session (`i...<esc>`) are programmatic (non-user-input), but you _did_ send actual user-input _during_ the session.\n> \n> And it turns out there is already a global (`maptick`) which lets us detect detect this kind of thing. So `CmdAtom` can capture an insert-session if there was user-input during the session, even if its delimiters were not \"user input\".\n> \n> The `vim.schedule` is a red herring in this case, since it only \"enters\" an insert-session.\n\nIn other words, for `CmdAtom`, the action `<C-j>` is simply considered a programmatic exit from insert-session(non-user-input)(`<Esc>`), so the Atom ends at this point, and anything that happens afterward is irrelevant to that Atom.\n\nBecause the `feedkey (\"i\")` programmatically triggers entry into insert-session, it is not considered an Atom, causing subsequent duplicate `<C-j>` to become invalid. Even if  `<C-j>dsaffsfafafdadf`, `dsaffsfafafdadf` will be ignored.",
